\chapter{Sockets for Programs}
\label{ch:sockets}

Until now a program could use the network only through private system calls (\texttt{tcp\_connect}, \texttt{dns\_resolve}, \ldots, chapter
\ref{ch:tls}). Programs that were written for Unix, such as the Python interpreter, call \texttt{socket}, \texttt{connect}, \texttt{sendto},
\texttt{recvfrom}, \texttt{poll} and \texttt{getaddrinfo} of the C library, and musl turns these into the Linux system calls 198 to 210 and 73 (\texttt{ppoll}).
This chapter is the kernel side (issue \#37): a socket is one more kind of file, next to files, devices, pipes and the files that the kernel makes up. It
reuses the kernel's own network stack of chapter \ref{ch:network} (\texttt{tcp\_connect}, \texttt{tcp\_read}, \texttt{udp\_send}, \ldots), which was written for
the kernel's own use and is polled: a program that waits for the network calls \texttt{net\_poll\_once} itself, as the private calls do.

\paragraph{What is there.} IPv4 only. A \emph{stream socket} (\texttt{SOCK\_STREAM}) is a TCP connection that the program opens with
\texttt{connect}; a \emph{datagram socket} (\texttt{SOCK\_DGRAM}) is a UDP port, \texttt{bind} chooses it (or the first \texttt{sendto} gets
one) and it keeps the last four datagrams that arrive. \texttt{read} and \texttt{write} work on both (a stream, or a connected datagram socket), \texttt{send},
\texttt{recv}, \texttt{sendto}, \texttt{recvfrom} too, \texttt{poll} and \texttt{select} ask which sockets (and pipes) can be read or written
without waiting, a socket can be non-blocking (\texttt{SOCK\_NONBLOCK}, \texttt{O\_NONBLOCK}), \texttt{shutdown}, \texttt{getsockname}, \texttt{getpeername},
\texttt{setsockopt} and \texttt{getsockopt} exist (the options are accepted and ignored, \texttt{SO\_ERROR} and \texttt{SO\_TYPE} are answered).

\paragraph{What is not there.} \texttt{listen} and \texttt{accept} (a server in a program, issue \#124, is another piece of work and has its own calls), the
other families (\texttt{AF\_INET6}, \texttt{AF\_UNIX}: \texttt{EAFNOSUPPORT}, which is what a resolver and \texttt{socketpair} users expect to handle), raw sockets,
out-of-band data, \texttt{MSG\_PEEK}, ancillary data, \texttt{sendmsg}/\texttt{recvmsg}. A \texttt{bind} of a stream socket is remembered (the local port of the
later connection) and nothing else.

\section{Where it fits: one more kind of file}

A socket is file system number 6, and the number inside it is the index in a table of 16 sockets, like a pipe (chapter \ref{ch:pipes}). The same four
wrappers of the book's \texttt{u\_read}, \texttt{u\_write}, \texttt{u\_close} and \texttt{u\_reopen} (in \texttt{pipes.c}) pass the calls on to this file.
\texttt{fork} copies a descriptor with \texttt{u\_reopen}: the child gets one more user of the same socket (\texttt{refs}), as with a pipe end, and the socket goes away when
the last user has closed it.

