\subsection{Is it ready? (for \texttt{poll} and \texttt{select})}

The bits are the ones of \texttt{poll}: \texttt{POLLIN} 1, \texttt{POLLOUT} 4, \texttt{POLLERR} 8, \texttt{POLLHUP} 16. A stream socket can be read when data
has arrived or the other side has closed, and written when the connection is up and the send buffer has room; a connection that has failed has \texttt{POLLERR} and \texttt{POLLHUP}.
A datagram socket can be read when a datagram is queued and can always be written.

<<sockets.c>>=
int sock_ready (int gfd) {
  struct sock *s = sock_of (gfd);
  if (!s) return 32;                                                   // POLLNVAL
  if (s->type == SOCK_DGRAM_) return (s->qcount ? 1 : 0) | 4;
  if (s->listening) return tcp_pending (s->local_port) ? 1 : 0;           // a connection is waiting
  if (s->local) return (LCOUNT (s) || s->eof ? 1 : 0) | (s->peer_gone ? 1 | 4 | 16 : (LCOUNT (&socks[s->peer]) < LBUF ? 4 : 0));
  if (!s->c) return 4 | 16;                                            // not connected (any more): POLLOUT | POLLHUP, like Linux
  if (tcp_id (s->c) != s->id) return 8 | 16;
  if (s->connecting) { if (tcp_established (s->c)) { s->connecting = 0; return 4; }  return tcp_closed (s->c) ? 8 | 16 : 0; }
  return (tcp_readable (s->c) ? 1 : 0) | (tcp_writable (s->c) ? 4 : 0) | (tcp_closed (s->c) ? 16 : 0);
}
